A1. The road to this place is also paved with good inventions. ~ HELL
A4. This is over when the bills exceed the coos. ~ HONEYMOON
A11. Synthetic gin makes a married man see double and feel this way. ~ SINGLE
A16. The only thing most bachelors learn how to sew. ~ OAT
A17. The first man to eat an oyster. ~ HERO
A18. What a man does when his flivver doesn't run smoothly. (The man in question is not Mr. Dawes.) ~ OILS
A19. A bird's fin. ~ WING
A20. An awful state to be in (abbr.). ~ IA
A21. Something hard to get over (abbr.). ~ MT
A22. The kind of predicament to keep out of. ~ DIRE
A23. If you're Scotch you can turn your money into this without studying alchemy. ~ FUND
A24. Where one eye's better than two. ~ WINK
A25. Distance between the sun and the earth. ~ FAR
A26. What the fattest porker in the pen came to at last. ~ LARD
A27. To engage for temporary service. (Like getting a cook). ~ HIRE
A28. Substitute hold-ups. ~ PINS
A29. To roll up and fasten to something like a flag or sail. ~ FURL
A30. Yankee liquid lightning. ~ BOOZE
A32. An Elizabethan kiss. ~ BUSS
A33. America's last resort. ~ CAFE
A34. Fidgety. ~ FUSSY
A35. A form of plant life found on cultivated rose bushes. ~ ROSE
A36. The number of American wives who can out-talk their husbands. (This is a very conservative estimate). ~ MOST
A37. What a small boy usually hurls at a little smaller boy. ~ DARE
A38. The destination of all Sunday golfers. ~ HOLE
A39. Egyptian Zoological Society (abbr.). ~ EZS
A40. The way of the transgressor is as hard as any other well beaten one. ~ PATH
A41. The way blind dates generally turn out. ~ PUNK
A42. What fuzzy flappers keep their hair in. ~ CURL
A43. The Spanish spinster's answer. ~ SI
A44. You and I. ~ WE
A45. All things come to those who order this. ~ HASH
A46. What firemen are overcome by (sing.). ~ FUME
A47. Enamored. ~ FOND
A48. Sherman's synonym for Satan's sanctum sanctorum. ~ WAR
A49. The wages of sin are paid in this if you're a politician or a bootlegger. ~ CASH
A50. An anarchist's persuader. ~ BOMB
A51. A piece of French pastry. ~ TART
A52. An old one is never married. ~ MAID
A53. What snowballs do in hades. ~ MELT
A54. A good place to go if you're looking for real live chickens. ~ COOP
A55. A married man may be the captain of his soul, but he always has one of these who does the steering. ~ MATE
A56. A galvanized descendant of the old oaken bucket. ~ PAIL
A57. Your grandmother's daughter. (Not mother.) ~ AUNT
A58. A prime essential for a reel romance. ~ GIRL
A59. It's a strong one who has no yearning. ~ JANE
A60. Something a toad has that peaches don't have. ~ WART
A62. In French and Latin this is "is." ~ EST
A63. A sheepfold. ~ COTE
A64. What a parlor was in grandfather's day when the lights were turned of. ~ DARK
A65. Nothing divided by two. ~ KISS
A66. Of all my wife's relations I love this one the best. ~ ME
A67. In regard to (abbr.). ~ RE
A68. Dora thinks this is what people do when they go on sleighing parties. ~ SLAY
A69. What a Russian is when the thought of work moves him to tears. ~ LAZY
A70. This is as good as a mile, and also good for just about a mile. ~ MISS
A71. The only thing that keeps us (from) drinking. (Correct this sentence!) ~ LAW
A72. There's no such word. (Not "can't.") ~ FAIL
A73. What a bee says before he sits down on you. (Not excuse it, please!) ~ BUZZ
A74. What careful Cal would say, if anything, if his hobby horse threw him. ~ OUCH
A75. There are a great many letters in this! ~ MAIL
A76. What heavy Hesters prefer to be called. ~ PLUMP
A78. The lid of the Teapot of Sinclair and Fall. ~ DOME
A79. Magnitude of material substance. (Nuthin' tricky here!) ~ BULK
A80. The breath of one addicted to Jamaica. ~ RUMMY
A81. What benedicts do when they start an argument with friend wife. ~ LOSE
A82. Every year is this kind of year for pedestrians. ~ LEAP
A83. The host who entertains with the family photograph album. ~ BORE
A84. Thumpthing you do when you pronounth thethe wordth. ~ LISP
A85. An English dumb-bell. ~ ASS
A86. Some people get this way from toil and others get crooked trying to avoid it. ~ BENT
A87. Close relatives of pots. ~ PANS
A88. The food that made Friday famous. ~ FISH
A89. To, or not to, that is the question. ~ BE
A90. The direction New York is from New Orleans (abbr.). ~ NE
A91. They generally go with kisses. ~ HUGS
A92. What pieces of purple carbon paper do when placed in a flame. ~ BURN
A93. The chief ingredient of most campaign speeches. ~ BUNK
A94. What you get when your girl takes an hour "to be down in a minute." ~ MAD
A95. These happen in even the best of regulated ball games. ~ ERRORS
A96. JUDGE named 'em this because they border on the humerus. ~ FUNNYBONE
A97. Things they used to flirt over in the good old days. ~ FANS

D1. These are being made in the cellar still. ~ HOMEBREWS
D2. What Marie Antoinette liked to do at mealtime. ~ EAT
D3. A shavetail (army abbr.). ~ LT
D4. What Elks generally travel in (sing.). (This has nothing to do with patrol wagons or excursion trains.) ~ HERD
D5. This is rather crude and unrefined. ~ ORE
D6. What wives mean when they say no. ~ NO
D7. This goes with truly in many letters. ~ YOURS
D8. A place where gold-diggers are usually found. This is deep stuff. ~ MINE
D9. What nearly all gardeners will eventually grow. ~ OLD
D10. The Latin crossword bone. ~ OS
D11. It's a sin to commit these. ~ SINS
D12. Something that runs out of the pen. ~ INK
D13. No good (abbr.). ~ NG
D14. Guys who keep saying, "You're the first girl I ever kissed." ~ LIARS
D15. In apartment houses this is the bird who gets the morning paper (two words). ~ EARLYBIRD
D17. What a string of rowboats at the end of a dock are for. ~ HIRE
D19. The first of a famous trinity found in cabarets. ~ WINE
D22. The "days" of the barrooms. ~ DAZE
D23. Something people lose when they open their hands. ~ FIST
D24. A man isn't necessarily a bigamist because he has one too many of these. ~ WIFE
D25. When this blows out, it leaves people in the dark. ~ FUSE
D26. One man's this is another man's gain. ~ LOSS
D27. Shut up! (High-brow.) ~ HUSH
D28. The best thing to do, if you can't drive one-handed. ~ PARK
D29. The one word toward which the gasoline indicator never seems to point. ~ FULL
D31. To slowly percolate. ~ OOZE
D32. The two of 'em. ~ BOTH
D33. A safe thing to walk with on Sunday afternoon. ~ CANE
D34. Four out of five have it. ~ FORD
D36. Incipient home-brew. ~ MASH
D37. The kind of bell whose tongue never makes the right sound at the right time. ~ DUMB
D38. What all hunters do at some time or other. ~ HUNT
D40. This is past. ~ PAST
D41. An implement that is a necessity to dairy farmers. ~ PUMP
D42. An apple's spinal column. ~ CORE
D43. What Columbus had to do to discover America. ~ SAIL
D45. The cry of the lonely sentinel. ~ HALT
D46. But you can't do it to all of the people all of the time! ~ FOOL
D47. Something no man can control. (Nothing to do with wives or flivvers.) ~ FATE
D48. What most waitresses should learn to do. ~ WAIT
D49. You can buy most any judge if you have fifteen of these (sing.). ~ CENT
D50. You wouldn't mind the mosquitoes so much if they didn't do this. ~ BORE
D51. A guy who can hold three quarts of Gordon's Dry. ~ TANK
D52. The god of war. ~ MARS
D53. To think closely in meditative silence. ~ MUSE
D54. This has outskirts and a heart. ~ CITY
D55. A girl who was quite contrary. (One of the many.) ~ MARY
D56. Small boys think their teacher's motto is, "They shall not do this." ~ PASS
D57. Good 'till the last drop! ~ AEROPLANE
D58. One from the field counts three points. ~ GOAL
D59. Paul Whiteman's kingdom. ~ JAZZ
D60. If this were a horse, a beggar might ride. ~ WISH
D61. There are enough bum jokes about these now without making any more wisecracks. (Two words.) ~ NEWLYWEDS
D63. To cut short. ~ CLIP
D64. What a blow on the head leaves you in. ~ DAZE
D65. There's more than one in a gallon of moonshine. ~ KICK
D66. What reckless drivers do to thousands of not so nimble pedestrians every year. ~ MAIM
D68. Identical. ~ SAME
D69. The kind of sugar the Prince of Wales uses in his tea. ~ LUMP
D70. A very stubborn animal. ~ MULE
D71. One thing lovers hate to see go on in the parlor. ~ LAMP
D72. What all girls do if you're two minutes late. ~ FUSS
D73. What the Pilgrims employed to land on Plymouth Rock. ~ BOAT
D74. This belongs to both you and me. ~ OURS
D75. All love-making. ~ MUSH
D77. The one who has never been known to win in a poker game. ~ LOSER
D78. Inhabited by lions, writers and robbers. ~ DENS
D79. A Scotchman's opinion of all Scotch lassies. ~ BONNY
D80. A dangerous thing to run. ~ RISK
D82. What chorus girls keep in silk stockings. ~ LEGS
D83. A rural garage. ~ BARN
D84. Something that's always busy. ~ LINE
D86. A plant seed that's liable to get quite attached to you. ~ BUR
D87. A form of wit favored by wits, half-wits, and nit-wits. ~ PUN
D88. You'll find plenty of this in a copy of JUDGE. ~ FUN
D89. Reformers' delight. ~ BAN
D91. A brand of oatmeal. ~ HO
D92. A co-educational institution of Boston (abbr.). ~ BU
D93. A knight of the road. ~ BO
D94. Manufacturer of old-fashioned pie and coffee. ~ MA


